33	/**
34	 * The Transfers resource, under the {@code /api} prefix the security chain opens by name.
35	 *
36	 * <p>It holds no repository — the boundary {@code ModuleBoundariesHoldTest} enforces — and
37	 * no rule of its own: what a request may say is declared as constraints on
38	 * {@link CreateTransferRequest}, and every refusal a Transfer can meet is raised where the
39	 * facts to decide it are, under the lock. What this class adds is the last step, below:
40	 * which status code each of those refusals is worth.
41	 *
42	 * <p><b>The refusal handlers sit on the controller rather than in a {@code
43	 * @ControllerAdvice}</b>, where they are reached by the structure of the resolver's lookup.
44	 * An advice would instead have to out-order {@code ProblemDocumentAdvice}, whose handler for
45	 * {@code Exception} would otherwise answer every refusal below as a {@code 500} (design
46	 * decision 14).
47	 *
48	 * <p>The path is written out again in the tests that call it, rather than read from the
49	 * constant below, on {@code AccountController}'s reasoning: a renamed path is a breaking
50	 * change for the generated frontend types, and a test reading the same constant as the
51	 * mapping would follow the rename silently instead of failing on it.
