274		/**
275		 * The one failure this endpoint answers that is nobody's fault on this side of the wire,
276		 * and the only {@code 5xx} it raises deliberately. {@code 503} rather than {@code 500}
277		 * because the distinction is the whole message: the request was fine, this service is
278		 * fine, and a third party it depends on is not — so coming back later is worth doing,
279		 * which the {@code Retry-After} says in the machine-readable half.
280		 *
281		 * <p><b>The Idempotency Key is left {@code FAILED} by the port</b>, so the retry this
282		 * invites executes properly rather than replaying this failure. That is what makes the
283		 * lean retry policy of ticket 25 a complete answer rather than a give-up: three attempts
284		 * inside one request, and the client's own retry after them.
285		 *
286		 * <p>The pair and the attempt count are reported because "we asked three times" is the
287		 * difference between a blip this application already absorbed and an outage it never had
288		 * a chance against — and the pair is the provider's failure rather than the caller's
289		 * Accounts, which is why the members are named for the quote and not for the Transfer.
290		 */
291		@ExceptionHandler
292		ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail> handleExchangeRateUnavailable(ExchangeRateUnavailableException outage) {
293			ProblemDetail problem = problemOf(HttpStatus.SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE,
294					ProblemType.FX_PROVIDER_UNAVAILABLE, FX_UNAVAILABLE_DETAIL);
295			problem.setProperty("baseCurrency", outage.getBase());
296			problem.setProperty("quoteCurrency", outage.getQuote());
297			problem.setProperty("attempts", outage.getAttempts());
298			return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE)
299					.header(HttpHeaders.RETRY_AFTER, FX_RETRY_AFTER_SECONDS)
300					.body(problem);
301		}
